        "story": "Are your parents still in town?\" I can ask friendly, nonthreatening questions. He shifts, though, like I've asked something hard. \"I mean, maybe. I don't really know much about them.\" \"Oh,\" I say, unsure what to do with this information. Cliff shrugs, an attempt to brush away the tension, but it's not convincing. \"My grandma raised me. She actually worked at the gas station right over there.\" He gestures out the window toward the Fas Gas across the street. \"I'd get free hot dogs when they were on the spinners too long to sell, so it wasn't too shabby, as long as you don't care about meat temperature danger zones.\" I try to imagine a child-sized Cliff with messy golden-honey hair and skinned knees, sitting across the street with his grandma eating expired hot dogs.",
